A||||0||theogre||87-88 4 cyl VSS feed to ECM.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||11-06-2000||08:26 PM||||Ok, Here’s one we need to work on confirming, and we can't just rely on GM shop books.<P>As I noted in another post awhile back the DIS 4 cyl appears to have a change to the ECM & Speedometer VSS feeds. According to documents from ALLdata, the VSS generator is now fed directly to the ECM, which now contains a divider/buffer. Other people have said the VSS still goes to the buffer before heading to the ECM. I have already confirmed the presence of the divider/buffer within the ECM. We now need to confirm if/how that buffer is being used.<P>What I want to know is this--- Does anyone have an 87-88 DIS 4 cyl Fiero that’s already apart or otherwise not likely to be driven? (I don’t want to risk damaging good ECM connectors.) What color wires are at the following locations on the ECM connectors? Knowing what wires are there tells us if the VSS is now connected to the ECM instead of a buffer in the dash.<P>Connector color, Terminal #, wire color & what it does.<P>Black, 6, Solid Purple if VSS generator is connected <BR>Black, 13, Solid Yellow if VSS generator is connected<P>White, 23, Solid Brown if fed from external buffer.<P>The terminals are counted from 1-12 then back up from 13-24. 1 & 24 are on the same end of the connector. (The wires are also the only ones of those colors on the connector.)<P>Pin 1 on the black connector (B1) will have an orange wire in it. (Serial Data)<BR>Pin 2 on the black connector (B2) will have a purple wire in it. (O2 senosr)<P>Pin 1 on the white connector (W1) will have a dark blue wire in it. (Injector drive)<P><BR>B6 would be in the middle of the black connector on the opposite side from the latch.<BR>B13 is on the latch side, diagonally opposite pin 1.<P>If B6 and B13 are wired then W2 may have a red wire and W22 may have a dark green wire. These are the 2000 & 4000 ppm outputs that should feed the dash when the VSS is connected to the ECM. Are either or both these wires present?<P><BR>This information is important in a couple ways. First it determines what PROM you can use. I’ve found out that the VSS signal selection is done thru a flag in the PROM. The means you can only use PROMs from another 87-88 Fiero or from a 6000 acording to ALLdata. Second it effects how you hook up an aftermarket cruise control.<P>BTW, Adding factory cruise to the DIS cars may not be as hard as we all thought. The ECM is already wired for it. It’s now just an issue of determining what other things get plugged into the ECM’s cruise connector. (Small square black connector hanging out of the main harness at the back of the console frame.) It looks like the thing isn’t to bad to hook up. Shouldn’t be that much more work than adding an aftermarket unit.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||1
